olicy Engine (PE)The Policy Engine is the decision-making core of a Zero Trust Architecture. It evaluates all relevant security signals — such as user identity, device postureetwork location, and risk level — before determining whether access to a resource should be allowed or denied. The PE does not directly enforce access; instead, it analyzes policies and contextual data to make a real-time authorization decision. It functions as the “brain” of the system, ensuring that every access request is verified before trust is granted.🔹 Policy Administrator (PA)The Policy Administrator acts as the coordinator that carries out the decision made by the Policy Engine. Once the PE determines whether access should be granted or denied, the PA establishes or blocks the session accordingly. This may include issuing authentication tokens, configuring secure connections, or instructing enforcement points on how to handle the request. While it does not decide policy itself, it ensures the decision is properly executed within the environ🔹 Policy EnfThe Policy Enforcement Point is the component that physically controls access to the protected resource. It sits in front of the application, database, or system and either allows or blocks traffic based on instructions from the Policy Administrator. The PEP acts as the “gatekeeper,” ensuring that unauthorized users cannot bypass security controls. It continuously enforces the access decision
